00:00:22.696 --> 00:00:25.420
PROFESSOR: OK, so this is
the second part of the class,

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:28.470
and this is the last
part of the semester.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.860
So I think we've traveled a lot.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:35.730
You know, we've seen so many
difficult issues, I think.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.390
And I want to give you credit
for, throughout this semester,

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:42.980
for being so open, honest,
and being able to engage

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:45.310
in difficult issues.

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:47.280
And each time that you
guys are presenting,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.170
you might remember
me nagging you

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:52.890
to get deeper into
personal issues

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.210
and not just the abstract.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.245
And the reason is
because, I think

00:00:56.245 --> 00:00:57.870
you make a better
case for whatever you

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:01.650
want to argue when you read
your own biography into it.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.530
And I think overall it worked.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:07.680
I myself, you know, learned so
much throughout the semester

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.450
from listening to some of
your own personal discoveries

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:14.520
about yourself, about issues
of race, language, gender,

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:15.870
sexuality.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.540
And I give you lots
of credit for that.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:19.610
It's risky.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.110
And because before
you came here you

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:22.818
didn't know each other,
obviously, right?

00:01:22.818 --> 00:01:26.205
And yet to be able to open
yourself to difficult questions

00:01:26.205 --> 00:01:30.767
and to give answers that
were so thoughtful and so--

00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:32.850
many of them, I think, I
didn't expect some of you

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:36.800
would, you know, literally come
out in this class, in a way,

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:37.605
right?

00:01:37.605 --> 00:01:40.150
So I thought that was
very brave of you.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:44.610
So I give you my
respect for that.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:47.720
So with that coming
to end, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.735
[LAUGH]

00:01:49.735 --> 00:01:52.690
So we can-- let's do
[INAUDIBLE] that many--

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:54.820
if there are things
that you feel

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.598
you will take with
you to the next step,

00:01:57.598 --> 00:02:00.140
or that you feel that others
who come after you in this class

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:05.100
might benefit from, anything you
want to share of your learning

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.226
experience in this class.

00:02:07.226 --> 00:02:10.320
And how you think
you can bridge those

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.800
gaps that we've seen, either
in our societies or families.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.910
So I wasn't here last Thursday,
but I watched the video

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:19.948
before class.

00:02:19.948 --> 00:02:21.990
And there too, like I was
actually very impressed

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:26.405
by how you guys brought
up personal issues.

00:02:26.405 --> 00:02:29.076
But there is a lot
there to work on.

00:02:29.076 --> 00:02:30.525
So [INAUDIBLE].

00:02:30.525 --> 00:02:35.150
For this class, for these
last 40 minutes or so,

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:37.410
you can have a big
something to bring out.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:40.600
What would it be?

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:41.580
You ready to get past?

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:43.856
We can go to [? Edna. ?]

00:02:43.856 --> 00:02:45.350
AUDIENCE: It's
quicker with words.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:45.850
Better.

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:46.840
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.810
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:52.110
AUDIENCE: So I'm
not sure if I fully

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:53.610
understand what I'm
supposed to say,

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.958
but I'll say what
I was thinking.

00:02:55.958 --> 00:02:57.750
So for me personally
throughout this class,

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:01.890
I think I realized I like
explored myself and my identity

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:04.470
way more, and I think I was
supposed to ask questions about

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:06.948
myself that I hadn't.

00:03:06.948 --> 00:03:08.490
And for me, the main
thing I realized

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:12.360
is like, how uncomfortable
I am with being multiracial.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.100
Like I enjoy being
multiracial, but I

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.710
realize that there's parts
of me like that don't really

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:20.490
fit together, and how like
I don't really see myself

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:22.930
as one certain race or ever.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:25.530
And I think, you know, the
things that helped with that

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:28.420
were like, you know,
our personal essays,

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:31.100
and like definitely
the free writing

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:33.700
assignments that one of the
guest professors had us do.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:35.257
Like that got pretty deep.

00:03:35.257 --> 00:03:36.840
And like I was kind
of like, oh, crap,

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.430
I have to write at like
9:30 in the morning

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:39.472
when she first said that.

00:03:39.472 --> 00:03:42.390
But then like I really
enjoyed it as it went on.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:44.700
And I think like
maybe bringing out

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.970
more multiracial
topics next year

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:49.630
would be beneficial
to people like me,

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:55.180
like who didn't know that was
like a problem with themselves.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:57.180
And I think like also a
really interesting topic

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:58.660
that we went over was like--

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.650
I think what do we call,
fake-- no, passing.

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:03.030
Passing.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:06.390
And like how that's
a privilege and like

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:08.680
talking about all the
controversial issues on that.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:10.140
And I think I
really also thought

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:11.980
about that for this class.

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:13.580
So that's something
I benefited from.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:14.370
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:18.230
So you said faking
instead of passing.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:20.730
And that remind me of a comment
that Cynthia made back then,

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:21.230
right?

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:23.460
That often when we
think of passing we

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.632
think of it as something
which is negative.

00:04:26.632 --> 00:04:29.090
But I think [INAUDIBLE] that
point, which I knew very well.

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:31.952
It was very important
that the notion of passing

00:04:31.952 --> 00:04:33.660
should not be seen
from that perspective.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.160
And there's a way
that one can think

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:39.870
of passing as a normal
survival strategy, in a way.

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:42.760
So I think we ought to
think of it like-- you

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:44.010
don't have to talk about that.

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:45.254
But you can--

00:04:45.254 --> 00:04:46.625
[LAUGH]

00:04:46.625 --> 00:04:48.680
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:49.680
PROFESSOR: Say it again?

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:51.210
AUDIENCE: Nothing's-- can
I have another moment?

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.335
PROFESSOR: Sure, of course.

00:04:52.335 --> 00:04:54.181
We can go to [? Burke ?]
and then come back.

00:04:54.181 --> 00:04:56.983
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:59.698 --> 00:05:01.240
AUDIENCE: So I guess
in terms of our,

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:04.070
I think, it takes a lot of
reflecting and trying to think

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.200
about all that we've discussed.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:09.200
But just sort of a quick note.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.450
One of the things that this
class has sort of solidified

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:16.300
for me is the importance
of conversations.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:18.960
And so I think most of
the learning that I've

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:24.970
done in this class and other
sort of seminar based classes

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:26.560
sort of pales in
comparison-- or what

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:27.730
I learned in my
technical courses

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:29.140
pales in comparison
to the things

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:32.080
that I've learned in
classes like this.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.290
Because at the end of the day,
I think most of the learning

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:38.920
comes from those around you,
your classmates, the set

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:43.470
of experiences that they have,
more so than what you learn

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:45.076
in these technical classes.

00:05:45.076 --> 00:05:47.945
There's a saying that says like,
five years after you graduate,

00:05:47.945 --> 00:05:51.630
you won't remember 90%
or whatever of what

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:53.420
you learned in college.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:55.430
But I think what
you do remember is

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:57.740
these sort of conversations
that boil down

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:01.170
to personal experiences.

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:04.790
And so I think, again,
there is challenge

00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:06.950
in this world of
sort of approaching

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:12.320
people who aren't really willing
to have these conversations.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:14.940
But sort of changing
yourself is the first step.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:18.950
And I think if everyone sort of
makes or has this realization,

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:23.150
takes a step towards making this
change, it's very idealistic.

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:26.030
But I think the world
would be a better place.

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:28.126
I think that's probably
one thing that I've

00:06:28.126 --> 00:06:29.500
gotten from this class.

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:34.660
AUDIENCE: Whoa, man.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:41.020
Bouncing off what
[? Burke ?] said,

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:44.320
I think one way which I'd
like to see this class grow

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:48.160
is the integration of having
some sort of conversation,

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:51.130
like open discussion
instead of debate,

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:58.410
with someone who does not come
from a similar theological

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:04.840
or ideological background
as most people in this class

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:06.880
to try to see--

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:10.390
to sort of gain practice
in having discussions

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:12.580
with someone who's a
lot different from you

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:16.480
and developing a
common trust and coming

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:18.970
to see each other's
viewpoints in a way that

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:24.130
would be more
informative than what

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:27.526
one could do among a set
of like-minded individuals.

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:35.520
AUDIENCE: So one thing that
I enjoyed about this class,

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.520
I can distinctly
remember one class that

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:39.990
literally blew my mind.

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:42.540
I think I turned to somebody
and was like, oh my gosh.

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:45.010
And that was when
we interrogated

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:47.760
what MIT was as an
institution and where we sit

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:51.430
in this hierarchy of social--

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:55.170
in the social
hierarchy in the US.

00:07:55.170 --> 00:08:00.150
And I think coming to MIT I was
willing to give the institution

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:03.270
a free pass a lot because
I felt that I was getting

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:04.950
a lot from the experience.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:07.140
But I think now
that I'm a senior

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:11.218
and all the seniors here are--
last day of classes, you guys.

00:08:11.218 --> 00:08:13.260
Now that I'm at this point
where I can think back

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:16.830
on what MIT has done for me
and what and how it functions,

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:22.560
I think I'm better poised
to think more critically

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:24.930
about the institutions
I choose to join

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:29.030
in the future for postgraduate
education and after.

00:08:32.559 --> 00:08:35.200
AUDIENCE: So I took this class
as a challenge to myself.

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:38.470
Because at the beginning,
I knew about the prejudices

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:40.240
and stereotypes that
I had been taught

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:42.570
and I wanted to get rid of them.

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:44.965
And I think this class
has helped a lot with that

00:08:44.965 --> 00:08:47.590
just by hearing everyone else's
perspectives from the readings,

00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:50.650
from looking at the world--

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:54.310
by taking a step back and
looking at things as they are

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:58.460
and not how we were specifically
taught that they are.

00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:01.100
And I really appreciate that.

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:05.530
I want to, from here
on, move forward

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:11.060
and try to figure
out how to be an ally

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:12.820
from an Asian-American
perspective.

00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:16.990
Because a lot of the times,
Asian-American families

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:19.680
try to be the status quo.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.670
Like my parents have
always taught me to like

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:25.900
try to be, not
explicitly, but implicitly

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:27.970
try to be as white as you can.

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:31.750
And I think that that was
a really difficult lesson

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:34.510
to go through and then come
to a place like MIT which

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:37.060
is so diverse and which has
so many different backgrounds.

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:39.520
And to like come in thinking
there's a hierarchy where

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:41.290
there isn't, and
now going forward

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:44.977
trying to break that down.

00:09:44.977 --> 00:09:46.810
AUDIENCE: A lot of what
I was thinking about

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:49.480
during this class
for me personally,

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:51.070
was comparing and contrasting.

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:54.370
Because I come from a very--

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:57.070
I'm from Zimbabwe, so there's
not a lot of diversity.

00:09:57.070 --> 00:10:00.290
And this is an entirely
new experience to me.

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:03.520
So a lot of what I was doing was
comparing my past, my history,

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:06.970
my experiences, with
you guys' experiences

00:10:06.970 --> 00:10:08.140
and everything around me.

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:10.833
And thinking about, for
instance, in the readings,

00:10:10.833 --> 00:10:13.000
how they analyze different
situations, the situation

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:15.060
in America, the
situation in Haiti,

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:17.560
and thinking about where
these things are connected,

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:18.727
how they're different.

00:10:18.727 --> 00:10:19.810
What could have been done?

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:21.170
What should be done?

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:23.290
And I think--

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:26.560
I really thought about issues
like this before this class,

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:28.990
and now I'm starting
to think about it more.

00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:32.050
Especially in education, I'm
really interested in education

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:36.640
and how we can help teach
people in the future

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:38.673
how to use this skill
that they learn,

00:10:38.673 --> 00:10:40.090
how to think about
certain things,

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:43.360
how to approach certain problems
in a way that challenges

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:45.910
them and also challenges
the status quo

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:48.760
and challenges the
hierarchies that are set up.

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:51.640
So personally I think that
that this class definitely

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:54.040
helped me think
a lot more, and I

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:56.440
hope that in future will
help a lot more people think

00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:59.470
about their actions, think
about what they're saying,

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:01.300
think about what
they're doing, and think

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:03.430
about how they can
help and how they

00:11:03.430 --> 00:11:05.362
can engage their communities.

00:11:05.362 --> 00:11:06.820
For instance, I
remember when we're

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:11.350
talking about how we came to
MIT and we might graduate,

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:13.330
and then what are
you going to do

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:16.090
when you go back to the
communities where we come from?

00:11:16.090 --> 00:11:19.390
How do you plan to
affect those communities?

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:22.900
Do we go back as experts or
do we go back as one of them?

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:24.130
Do we try to fit in?

00:11:24.130 --> 00:11:25.930
Like questions like
that are definitely

00:11:25.930 --> 00:11:29.606
things that we should
think about and I think

00:11:29.606 --> 00:11:31.792
are very important.

00:11:31.792 --> 00:11:33.500
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I
think the biggest thing

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:37.310
for me was like learning
about what we were learning,

00:11:37.310 --> 00:11:39.620
but then thinking
back to how I grew up

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:41.060
in like a really white area.

00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:42.530
And I was always
taught like there

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:44.180
is one correct way
to speak, there

00:11:44.180 --> 00:11:48.890
is like one correct
way to teach kids.

00:11:48.890 --> 00:11:52.490
And thinking back now,
with all this information

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:56.780
on how damaging that really
is and how it's not really

00:11:56.780 --> 00:11:59.540
about the language and it's
not really about how we speak.

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:01.970
It's more about
marginalizing groups

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:04.150
that might speak that way.

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:08.450
And it's like just thinking
about how my entire school

00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:12.720
system was based on like
what they thought was correct

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:16.020
and was telling us what we
should think is correct.

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:20.870
And I think coming
out of this hopefully

00:12:20.870 --> 00:12:25.190
we can try to change some of
that in the school systems.

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:29.550
Because it can really affect
how kids view the world as they

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:30.050
grow up.

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:35.240
AUDIENCE: I think one thing
I gained from this class

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:40.670
is kind of, I guess,
verification of the idea

00:12:40.670 --> 00:12:43.010
that listening is
very important.

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:45.200
And it's kind of
various ways you

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:47.700
could think about it like just
in the context of this class,

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:51.680
whether it's like listening
to like other people share

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:54.350
their experiences and kind
of really taking it in

00:12:54.350 --> 00:12:56.900
and understanding
like how they fit in

00:12:56.900 --> 00:13:01.430
within whatever social hierarchy
or whatever kind of issue

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:03.950
we're discussing and seeing
how it's kind of played

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:04.880
a role in their lives.

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:08.000
And kind of seeing how you
could apply it to either better

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:09.650
someone else's
life, like improve

00:13:09.650 --> 00:13:13.070
your own kind of interaction
with the world in some way.

00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:16.490
Or whether it's listening
in terms of like language.

00:13:16.490 --> 00:13:19.190
You talked a lot about
language and kind

00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:22.530
of how certain modes of speaking
are somewhat marginalized.

00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:26.030
So ensuring that as you
go forward you really

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:29.030
listen to people regardless of
how they present their ideas

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:30.710
or present their thoughts.

00:13:30.710 --> 00:13:33.860
Or whether it's like listening
as far as engaging people who

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:35.660
have opposing opinions as you.

00:13:35.660 --> 00:13:38.240
Like really understanding
that in order

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:40.490
to strengthen your own
positions and really understand

00:13:40.490 --> 00:13:42.135
why it is that you
think the way it is,

00:13:42.135 --> 00:13:44.510
you have to be able to listen
to people who kind of think

00:13:44.510 --> 00:13:46.490
in a different way than you.

00:13:46.490 --> 00:13:49.040
So yeah, I've just seen a lot
of kind of different things

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:53.617
in this class that speaks
of importance of listening.

00:13:53.617 --> 00:13:55.450
AUDIENCE: I think what
